@user if used as a tool to aid work, sure, AI must be used. The problem occurs when it stops being a tool in the hands of professionals, and becomes an instrument to eliminate the professional figure in order to cut costs.
Using a calculator doesn't make you a mathematician, as using an AI to prompt a picture doesn't make you a painter.
And also, there is a big difference between doing a creative job, and being a calculator: a creative process must, of course, be creative, to invent, while doing 2+2 is a mechanical action, not a creative action.

@user I disagree, the degree to which it gets implemented is important. We are not talking right now, in the wide application of the system, as a tool to aid a professional (like it would be in the medicine field, for example), we're talking about an effort to cut costs by using generative AI to substitute an entire category of workers.

I think it is not a similar case to the steam engine, more like the horses getting substituted by cars, with the difference that we're talking about creative human work.

I'm not against AI used as a tool to aid work, such as generative AI used inside Photoshop to do photo comping, or used inside the creative process (but not to substitute the process itself), or such as AI used to guide surgeons in less invasive and safer procedures, I would be totally against AI used to substitute surgeons because they cost less (and are less efficient, such as in the case of creative works, where AI's quality for a professional is still really sub-par).

