@user Where you'll see AI being used to "cut costs" are mainly gonna be things like art, music, and writing. These are things that have little impact on public health and safety in the way replacing surgeons entirely would. I absolutely agree that AI isn't reliable enough to entirely replace surgeons, but it is coming very close to streamlining creators' access to things like custom art, music, and writing to use in other projects.

Like I've said, if "creative human work" still proves to be advantageous over AI generated creativity, it will still have its place. But there's nothing wrong with a low-impact field being substituted by a more accessible means. If the discussion shifts to using AI for fields that show clear risks to public health and safety, my mind would change to that end.

You use the phrase "cutting costs" as if it's inherently bad. Solo game devs, for example, would benefit greatly from being able to generate some idle background music or initial dialogue writing that fits their desired criteria as opposed to seeking out a composer or creative writer to hire, or sinking a large amount of time into trying to do it themselves and being dissatisfied with the result. If the end product is what they were seeking, it's a positive.

@user Chess is not at all comparable to the way LLMs work. Chess is fundamentally different in that the AI is capable of "training itself" - there's nothing about the chess AI that depends on human input, because we know how to (and can easily do so) objectively determine whether a game of chess is a win, draw, or loss. This means that an AI can play a game of chess against itself and learn to do things that lead to a win and avoid things that lead to a loss.

With a LLM it is different - the AI is fundamentally incapable of training itself, because the only thing a LLM is trying to do is mimic a human. which inherently makes it impossible to function without human input. There is no "success state" other than that it looks similar to what a human would write (and it also will have no notion of when there's a mistake in its training data - if there's any mistake in code it's trained on, it will repeat that mistake without a second thought about it). This means that the AI will learn all kinds of garbage patterns that technically match the data that's in its training set but doesn't work when you need to extrapolate it to try to solve things that are outside of the training data.

Chess AIs are driven by trying to win the game, LLMs are driven by trying to mimic humans - and it should really come as no surprise that if you design an AI with the only goal of mimicking humans that it will never surpass humans.. because any time it does anything better than a human, it will treat those things as mistakes too.

